good morning and welcome to the  Commission's press room today is  Thursday the 30th of January and it is  our pleasure to have with us executive  vice president miss tagger who will be  discussing with us an antitrust decision  effectively as president welcome well  thank you very much it's a pleasure to  be here today the Commission has decided  to find nbc/universal together with  other companies in the Comcast Group  fourteen point three million euros for  anti-competitive licensing and  distributing distribution practices  which are illegal under EU antitrust  rules NBCUniversal is a US company that  operates television networks and film  and TV producing companies all around  the world it also owns property rights  to the characters in its movies and the  themes the images and the logos from  those movies and it licenses hundreds of  companies in Europe to make and sell  products which uses that intellectual  property things like Jurassic Park toys  minions school bags and Shrek NOx in  return those licensees they pay  nbc/universal part of their sales  revenue as royalty you can find these  products in millions of Europeans homes  European home if you have children  that's a pretty good chance that it will  be in your home too NBC Universal  illegal behavior well that affected a  full range of those products from toys  and sweets to clothes and marks and  household  it's contracts with licensees divided up  the European single market and that took  away choice from consumers and may also  have effects its prices our  investigation found that nbc/universal  used contracts to set out which  countries these licenses could sell in  and which ones they were banned from in  some contracts NBC Universal also  limited their freedom to sell products  online it even gave some licensees a  list of customer customers that they  were allowed to sell to and these  restrictions meant that shops couldn't  freely choose which products actually to  order from different European  distributors a department store in Spain  couldn't sell and ET pajamas from a  Belgian manufacturer because that  manufacturer was banned from selling  into Spain so the extra-terrestrial may  have made it all the way to earth but  was banned from Spain because of  contractual restrictions meanwhile a  teenager in sweden couldn't buy himself  a big lebowski  t-shirts online so as a result of these  restrictions while consumers they lost  out on choice and probably also on lower  prices in short consumers retailers  licensees they were all deprived of the  benefits from the single market and  today's decision makes it clear that  brand owners cannot prohibit their  non-exclusive licensees from selling in  some Member States or to certain  customers or from selling online these  illegal behavior lasted for more than  six years started in the beginning of  2013 when nbc/universal increase  it's licensed thing activities in Europe  that was ahead of the launch of the  sequel to the various successful movie  Despicable Me it lasted until September  2019 when in the course of our  investigation  NBC told all its European licensees that  those anti-competitive sales  restrictions no longer applied and this  is why we have imposed a fine of  fourteen point three million euros NBC  Universal cooperates with the Commission  in our investigation it acknowledged the  infringement and provided additional  evidence and they did that relatively  early in the process as a result the  company was granted a 30% reduction of  the fine this sort of cooperation is now  a well-established antitrust procedure  in the last two years we have adopted  ten decisions involving companies that  has cooperated that have cooperated with  us and that is good news because it  means that we can enforce antitrust  rules faster and more efficiently so why  does it matter it's a small fine well  there are two good reasons first of all  because those this those restrictions is  all about merchandising the second one  is much broader this industry the  merchandising industry  well that is big business and it's  growing rapidly in Europe and around the  world and it's expected to keep growing  the merchandise sector in in Europe is  estimated to be worth around fifty  billion euros last year from a global  total of 230 billion euros and today's  decision is the third antitrust decision  which the Commission has taken in the  merchandise sector in less than a  year in March last year we find Nike for  a similar behavior including products  that carries Football Club brands like  FC Barcelona or Ventus and in July we  find Sanrio for a similar infringement  to do with the merchandise featuring  Hello Kitty and other characters these  three decisions they send a clear  message the commission will not tolerate  restrictions that undermine the single  market and that brings me to the second  reason why this decision is important  because the single market makes a vital  contribution to our prosperity it  provides a better deal for consumers and  more opportunities for businesses  today's decision is part of our drive to  make sure that the single market works  well the single market gives businesses  the potential to reach out to almost  half a billion consumers and  digitization has made it even easier for  consumers to buy and for businesses to  sell across borders in the European  Union and that ought to create  opportunities for businesses of course  including digital businesses to grow but  in practice there are still a number of  obstacles to sell across border even  today only one in five European  consumers buys online from retailers in  other member states so completing the  single market is an important part of  making Europe fit for the digital age  the obstacles to a for single market  comes in many form  including the contracts that stop  retailers from selling cross-border  within the European Union the sector  inquiry into e-commerce markets which we  carried out in the Commission's last  term well we found that territorial  restrictions they were actually becoming  increasingly common  both in contracts for the sale of  consumer goods online and for those  licensing digital products and this is  why it has been a priority for us to  tackle anti-competitive restrictions to  online and offline cross-border sales  for example we you find gas for limiting  the ability of distributors of its  clothes and accessories to sell  cross-border and online and we find  manufacturers of consumer electronics  for limiting cross-border sales for  pressuring their online retailers to  keep prices high with today's decision  the total fine with imposed on companies  for creating barriers to cross-border  trade in the single market comes to a  hundred and eighty four million euros  and this is since we completed the  sector inquiry in May 2017 this is  complemented by other parts of the  Commission's work take for example the  do blocking regulation which aims at  removing unjustified geo blocking when  they restricts online shopping so  today's decision is not just about  minions and dinosaurs and throws it's  about consumers and businesses in the  singer market it shows that the  Commission is committed to making every  effort to make sure that the single  market throughout Europe is working  better for us all thank you
 

